<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Exodus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Exodus is the second book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, recounting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>' departure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the giving of the law at Sinai. Its legal section, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-covenant">Book of the Covenant</a>, shows parallels with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a>. Scholars cite these parallels as evidence of the influence of the Babylonian Empire's legal tradition on later law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus.jpg" alt="Book of Exodus" width="240" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jusepe de Ribera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moses041.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
